    "bottomLine": "The default answer for a 5,000 to 7,500 watt portable generator with an L14-30 outlet and a short list of must-run circuits.",
    "description": "The 31406CRK is the transfer switch most electricians reach for when a homeowner shows up with a portable generator and asks for a safe way to connect it. Reliance builds it as a small prewired panel that mounts beside your main breaker panel, with a flexible conduit whip that enters the panel through a knockout. Six chosen circuits are moved onto it, and each gets a three-position switch: Line, Off and Gen. Because every circuit is switched individually, power from the generator can only go to those circuits and never back out onto the utility lines. The CRK suffix means the kit version, which adds Reliance's PB30 outdoor power inlet box and a 30-amp generator cord, so you plug the generator into the outside wall rather than running cords through a window. It is rated for 30-amp, 120/240-volt generators with an L14-30 outlet. Six circuits is enough for a furnace, refrigerator, freezer, sump pump and a couple of lighting or outlet circuits in a typical home."
  },
  {
    "id": "reliance-31410crk",
    "name": "Reliance Controls 31410CRK Pro/Tran Manual Transfer Switch Kit",
    "category": "30A, 10 circuits, 120/240V, includes inlet box and cord",
    "badge": "Best for More Circuits",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Ten circuit positions leaves room for 240-volt loads like a well pump",
      "Same per-circuit Line/Off/Gen switching as the six-circuit model",
      "Kit includes the PB30 inlet box and a 30-amp cord",
      "Lets you split loads across both legs of the generator more evenly",
      "Worth the step up when the six-circuit list is already full"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "More circuits does not mean more power; the generator is still the limit",
      "Larger enclosure needs more wall space next to the panel",
      "Easy to over-wire circuits you will never run at once"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it if your must-run list includes a 240-volt well pump or you simply have more than six essential circuits.",
    "description": "The 31410CRK is the ten-circuit sibling of the 31406CRK, built the same way: a prewired switch panel that sits next to your main panel, a whip into the panel, and a separate Line/Off/Gen switch for every circuit you move across. The reason to pay more is not power. Both kits are 30-amp, 120/240-volt units, and your generator's running watts still decide how much you can run at once. The reason is positions. A 240-volt load such as a well pump uses two of them, and once you add a furnace, refrigerator, freezer, sump pump, kitchen outlets and some lighting, six positions are gone before you get to the home office or the garage door opener. Ten positions also give you more freedom to balance 120-volt loads across the two legs of the generator, which keeps one side from overloading while the other loafs. Like the six-circuit kit, the CRK version adds the PB30 inlet box and a 30-amp cord."
  },
  {
    "id": "reliance-tf151w",
    "name": "Reliance Controls TF151W Furnace Transfer Switch",
    "category": "15A, single circuit, 120V, mounts at the furnace",
    "badge": "Best Transfer Switch for Furnace",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Purpose-built for one job: running a gas or oil furnace on a generator",
      "Mounts at the furnace instead of at the main panel",
      "Simple switch between utility and generator power",
      "Far less money and labor than a whole panel switch",
      "Works with smaller generators and inverter generators"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only powers one 15-amp, 120-volt circuit",
      "Your refrigerator, sump pump and lights still need another plan",
      "Some furnaces are fussy about generator power quality and grounding"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest legitimate way to keep the heat on in an outage if the furnace is the only thing you truly need.",
    "description": "In cold climates the most important load in an outage is usually the gas or oil furnace, which needs only a few hundred watts for its blower, inducer and controls but needs it hardwired. The TF151W exists for exactly that. It is a single-circuit, 15-amp, 120-volt transfer switch that replaces or sits beside the furnace's service switch, so the furnace can run either from its normal circuit or from a generator connected to the switch. There is no need to move circuits at the main panel and no need for a 240-volt generator, which makes it a good match for a small inverter generator. That is also its limit: it powers the furnace and nothing else. If you also want the refrigerator, freezer and sump pump, a six-circuit switch like the 31406CRK covers the furnace and those loads together, and costs less than buying several single-circuit switches. Have an electrician confirm it suits your furnace and local code."
  },
  {
    "id": "reliance-pb30",
    "name": "Reliance Controls PB30 Power Inlet Box",
    "category": "30A, L14-30 inlet, outdoor enclosure",
    "badge": "Best 30-Amp Generator Inlet Box",
