        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every outage season, someone plugs a generator into a dryer outlet with a homemade cord and energizes the line a utility crew is working on. A transfer switch exists so that never happens, and the right one for most homes is a modest kit plus a few hours of an electrician's time.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Generator Transfer Switch Does, and Why You Need One
          </h2>

          <p>
            A portable generator makes useful power, but it has no safe way to reach the furnace, the well pump or the refrigerator's dedicated circuit on its own. Extension cords cover a few plug-in appliances and nothing hardwired. A generator transfer switch is the piece that connects the generator to selected household circuits while guaranteeing those circuits are disconnected from the utility at the same moment. That last part is the whole point, and it is why a transfer switch or a listed interlock is required by the National Electrical Code for any generator connected to premises wiring.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason is simple and deadly. If generator power flows into your panel while the main breaker is on, it runs backward out to the street, through the pole transformer, and steps up to thousands of volts on the line a utility crew believes is dead. That is backfeeding, and it kills lineworkers and neighbors every year. It also lets utility power slam into your generator when the grid comes back, which can destroy the generator or start a fire. The so-called suicide cord, a cord with two male plugs used to feed a dryer or range outlet, is the classic way people do it. Never use one, under any circumstances.
          </p>

          <p>
            A properly installed transfer switch makes that impossible by design. Each switched circuit can be connected to the utility or to the generator, never both. Manual switches do it with a physical switch per circuit; interlock kits do it with a sliding plate that blocks the main breaker and the generator breaker from being on together; automatic switches do it with a motorized mechanism. All three are legitimate when installed correctly and listed for the job. What is not legitimate is anything that relies on you remembering to flip the main breaker first.
          </p>

          <p>
            A transfer switch also makes outages calmer. Instead of snaking cords through a cracked window, which lets exhaust in, you roll the generator out, plug one cord into an inlet box on the wall, start it, and move a few switches. Your furnace runs, your sump pump runs, your lights work on their normal switches. The generator itself still has to run outdoors, far from openings, and you still need working carbon monoxide alarms inside. A transfer switch fixes the wiring problem, not the exhaust problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have not yet chosen a generator, start with our pillar guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a>, then come back here. The switch has to match the generator's outlet and output, so picking the generator first saves buying the wrong kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Manual Transfer Switch vs Interlock Kit vs Automatic
          </h2>

          <p>
            A manual transfer switch, the kind Reliance sells as Pro/Tran, is a small prewired subpanel that mounts beside your main panel. An electrician moves your chosen circuits onto it, and each one gets its own Line/Off/Gen switch. During an outage you plug in the generator, start it, and flip only the circuits you want onto generator power. It is the most foolproof option for owners who will use it a few times a year, because you can see exactly which circuits are on the generator and cannot accidentally feed the whole house.
          </p>

          <p>
            An interlock kit takes a different approach. It is a metal plate or slide mounted on the main panel's cover that physically prevents the main breaker and a dedicated generator breaker from being on at the same time. The generator feeds the panel through that breaker from an outdoor inlet box. Because the whole panel becomes available, you manage loads by switching off the branch breakers you do not want and turning on the ones you do. Interlocks are usually cheaper than a manual switch and offer every circuit, but they demand more discipline during an outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch with interlocks is that they must be listed for your specific panel. Interlocks are not universal: the plate has to fit the exact panel model, breaker layout and cover, and the generator breaker must be secured as the listing requires. Panel manufacturers sell their own interlock kits for some models, and third-party kits are sold for others. An electrician will check the panel's label and the local inspector's position before installing one. Some jurisdictions are stricter about third-party interlocks than others, so ask before buying anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            An automatic transfer switch senses the outage, starts a standby generator, and moves the load without anyone touching it. That is the right answer for permanently installed home standby generators and for people who travel or have medical equipment that cannot wait. For a portable generator that has to be wheeled out and started by hand, automatic switching mostly adds cost without adding much. If you are leaning toward a standby system, our breakdown of <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">what a Generac standby installation costs</a> includes the transfer switch in the total.
          </p>

