        <div id="amps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 30-Amp L14-30 vs 50-Amp: Matching the Switch to the Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first spec to check on any generator transfer switch is the inlet and amperage, and that is decided by your generator's largest outlet. Most portable generators in the 5,000 to 9,000 watt range have a NEMA L14-30 outlet: a 30-amp, 120/240-volt, four-prong twist-lock. Larger portables often add a 50-amp, 125/250-volt California-style outlet, sometimes called a CS6369 outlet, that takes a cord with a CS6365 plug. Look at the generator's control panel; the outlet type is printed beside it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The amperage sets the ceiling. A 30-amp, 240-volt connection can carry about 7,200 watts in theory, and 30-amp switch kits like Reliance's are generally marketed for generators up to about 7,500 running watts. A 50-amp, 240-volt connection can carry up to 12,000 watts. In practice you should plan to run well under the ceiling, because motor starts surge far above running watts and generators do not like sitting at their limit for hours. Buying a 50-amp inlet for a generator with only an L14-30 outlet gains you nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch out for generators that are 120-volt only. Some smaller portables and many inverter generators have only 120-volt outlets, sometimes including an L5-30 or an RV-style TT-30. Those cannot feed both legs of a standard 120/240-volt switch the way an L14-30 can, and they cannot run any 240-volt load at all. Do not let anyone jumper the two legs together to make it work. A single-circuit furnace switch suits that kind of generator better, or talk to an electrician about what is allowed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cord has to match both ends. A 30-amp system uses an L14-30 plug at the generator and an L14-30 connector at the inlet, on a four-wire cord with 10-gauge conductors. A 50-amp system uses a much heavier cord. Adapters that turn a 50-amp outlet into a 30-amp cord exist, but they put you in a position where the cord and inlet are protected only by the generator's own breaker, so ask your electrician before using one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the inlet to the generator's biggest 240-volt outlet.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">L14-30 on the generator means a 30-amp inlet and switch. A 50-amp CS6369 outlet means a 50-amp inlet is worth considering, but only if the switch or interlock breaker is sized to use it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="furnace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Transfer Switch for Furnace: The Single-Circuit Option
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the only thing you truly need in a winter outage is heat, a transfer switch for furnace use is the simplest legitimate setup. A gas or oil furnace with a blower, inducer motor, igniter and control board typically needs a few hundred watts running, more at blower start. That is within reach of even a small inverter generator. The problem has never been power; it is that the furnace is hardwired, so there is nowhere safe to plug a generator in.
          </p>

          <p>
            A furnace transfer switch like the Reliance TF151W solves that at the furnace itself. It replaces or sits beside the furnace's service switch, and lets you flip the furnace from its normal circuit to a generator connection. No panel work, no 240-volt generator, and a much smaller electrician bill. People sometimes ask whether they can just put a plug on the furnace and use an extension cord. Some jurisdictions allow a cord-and-plug furnace connection and many do not, and the answer depends on the furnace's installation instructions, so let an electrician decide rather than improvising.
          </p>

          <p>
            Furnaces can be particular about generator power. Modern control boards sense the flame through a small current that depends on a solid neutral-to-ground reference, and some furnaces refuse to light on a generator with a floating neutral or with reversed polarity. Inverter generators produce clean power, which helps electronics, but the grounding question is separate. If the furnace locks out on generator power, that is an electrician's diagnosis, not a reason to start modifying cords or bonding plugs yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            A furnace-only switch is a narrow tool. It keeps the heat on, but the refrigerator, freezer, sump pump and lights still need another answer. If you want two or three of those as well, a six-circuit switch like the 31406CRK usually makes more sense than multiple single-circuit switches. If you are also looking at plug-in heat as a backup, our guide to the <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">best indoor heaters for a power outage</a> covers what is actually safe to run inside.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="loads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Load Management: Choosing Which Circuits Go on the Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A transfer switch does not make the generator any bigger. Whether you choose six circuits, ten, or the whole panel through an interlock, the generator's running watts decide what can run at the same moment. The job is to pick circuits that matter in an outage and that the generator can actually start. A furnace, refrigerator, freezer, sump pump, a few lighting circuits and the circuit that feeds your internet router cover most households.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the loads that cannot wait. A sump pump in a wet basement and a well pump in a rural house come first, then refrigeration, then heat, then lighting and communications. Well pumps and many sump pumps have large starting surges, so check the motor's rating plate or ask a pump installer. Our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-for-well-pump/">best generator for a well pump</a> walks through starting-watt math for 240-volt submersibles in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave the heavy resistive loads off. An electric water heater, electric range, electric dryer and central air conditioner can each swallow most or all of a portable generator's output. Putting them on the switch invites someone to turn them on mid-outage and trip the generator. If you want to know what your house actually draws, a whole-home monitor gives you real numbers instead of nameplate guesses; see our roundup of the <a href="/best-home-energy-monitor/">best home energy monitors</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Balance the two legs. A 120/240-volt generator delivers half its capacity on each 120-volt leg, so putting the furnace, refrigerator and freezer all on the same leg can overload that side while the other runs light. Electricians plan this when they assign circuits to the switch, and it is one of the better reasons to have a professional do the layout. Before the electrician arrives, use a <a href="/best-circuit-breaker-finder/">circuit breaker finder</a> to label your panel properly, which makes the circuit-selection conversation far faster.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Electrician, Permit and Inspection: How Installation Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installing a generator transfer switch is panel work, and panel work belongs to a licensed electrician. The main lugs in your panel stay energized even with the main breaker off, the service conductors upstream of the main have no protection at all, and a mistake can leave a switch that looks right but backfeeds. Most jurisdictions require a permit for the work and an inspection afterward, and your homeowner's insurance may care whether you have one if anything ever goes wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            A typical manual switch install goes like this. The electrician mounts the switch beside the main panel, runs its whip into a knockout, moves your chosen branch circuits onto the switch, mounts the inlet box on an outside wall, and runs cable between the two. An interlock install involves fitting the listed plate, installing the generator breaker, and running cable from the inlet box to that breaker. Either way, it is commonly a half-day job for a straightforward panel, longer if the inlet is far from the panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the electrician to ask about your generator. They need to know its outlet type, its running watts, and whether its neutral is bonded to the frame. Many portables ship with a bonded neutral, and some transfer switches do not switch the neutral, which can create a second neutral-to-ground bond and cause GFCI trips. The fix depends on the generator and the switch, and it is an electrician's call. Do not remove bonding straps or modify the generator yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some utilities also want to know you have a generator connected to your house. It costs nothing to ask, and in some areas it is required. While the electrician is there, it is worth adding a <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector</a>, since the panel is already open and generator power and grid restoration are both sources of voltage swings.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never backfeed through a dryer or range outlet.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A cord with male plugs on both ends energizes the utility lines and can kill a lineworker. It also leaves exposed live prongs on the loose end. No outage is worth it. If you need power before an electrician can come, run cords to individual appliances from a generator placed safely outdoors.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="operating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Operating the Switch Safely During an Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            The order matters, and it is worth taping a card with it inside the panel door. Set up the generator outdoors, far from windows, doors and vents, with the exhaust pointed away from the house. Connect the cord to the generator and then to the inlet box. Start the generator and let it warm up for a minute or two. Then switch circuits from Line to Gen one at a time, starting with the biggest motor load, so each start surge happens on its own instead of all at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            With an interlock, the sequence is a bit different. Turn off the main breaker, slide the interlock plate, and turn on the generator breaker. Then turn off most branch breakers and bring circuits back on one by one, watching the generator's load. This is where interlocks need more care than a manual switch: everything in the panel is available, so it is easy to switch on a water heater or dryer circuit by habit. Some owners label the must-run breakers in a bright color.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the grid comes back, reverse the process. Move each circuit back to Line, or turn off the generator breaker and slide the interlock back to restore the main, then shut down the generator and let it cool before unplugging. Never refuel a running or hot generator, and store fuel in approved containers away from the house. If you use a pump to move fuel from cans, our guide to the <a href="/best-fuel-transfer-pump/">best fuel transfer pumps</a> covers the types that suit gasoline.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generator placement is not optional. Carbon monoxide from a portable generator in a garage, crawlspace or on a porch kills people every outage season, even with doors open. Keep the generator outdoors and well away from the house, and have battery-backed CO alarms on every level. If you need power for medical equipment inside, a battery system such as those in our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">portable power station for home backup</a> guide can bridge the gap safely indoors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Generator Transfer Switch to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you have a portable generator with an L14-30 outlet and a short list of essentials, buy the Reliance 31406CRK. It covers six circuits, includes the inlet box and a cord, and is the setup most electricians already know how to install. For most houses with a gas furnace, a refrigerator, a freezer and a sump pump, six positions is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your list includes a 240-volt well pump or runs past six circuits, step up to the Reliance 31410CRK. The power ceiling is the same; the extra positions are what you are paying for. If your only real need is heat and your generator is small or 120-volt only, the Reliance TF151W furnace switch is the cheapest legitimate route, as long as your furnace and local code accept it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your electrician recommends an interlock for your panel, buy the interlock kit listed for that exact panel, plus the Reliance PB30 inlet box for a 30-amp generator or the PBN50 for a generator with a 50-amp outlet. Add the Reliance PC3020 cord when the 10-foot kit cord does not let you place the generator safely away from the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, budget for the electrician and permit, because they are not optional extras; they are what makes the hardware safe. And if you are still deciding on the generator, our pages on the <a href="/best-gas-generator/">best gas generators</a> and the <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">best small generators for home</a> cover the models that pair well with each switch. Laundry and kitchen outlets are not generator inlets; if you are dealing with a dryer outlet for its intended purpose, our guide to the <a href="/best-dryer-cord/">best dryer cord</a> covers matching cords to receptacles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a double-male cord into a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A suicide cord feeds generator power backward into the utility lines, where it can kill a lineworker, and leaves live exposed prongs on the free end. It is illegal and indefensible. If no transfer switch is installed yet, run extension cords to individual appliances instead, with the generator outdoors and well away from openings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an interlock not listed for your panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Interlock kits are panel-specific. A plate that fits a different panel model, or a universal kit an inspector will not accept, can fail inspection or fail to prevent both breakers being on. Have your electrician read the panel label and choose a kit listed for that exact panel before you order anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting more circuits to mean more power</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A ten-circuit switch does not make a 7,500-watt generator any stronger. The generator's running watts still decide what can run together. Choose circuits by priority, keep big resistive loads like water heaters off the switch, and stagger motor starts so the generator is not hit with every surge at once.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 50-amp inlet for a 30-amp generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The inlet should match the generator's largest 240-volt outlet. If the generator only has an L14-30, a 50-amp inlet box and cord add cost and weight without adding any usable power. Buy the 30-amp PB30 setup unless the generator actually has a 50-amp outlet and the switch can use it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the electrician and permit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Transfer switch installs involve the main panel, where some parts stay live even with the main off. A licensed electrician, a permit and an inspection are what make the system safe and insurable. DIY installs that look fine can still backfeed or create grounding faults that only show up during an outage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

