        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a transfer switch for a portable generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need one if you want the generator to power anything hardwired, such as a furnace, well pump or the circuits in your panel. The National Electrical Code requires a transfer switch or listed interlock for any generator connected to household wiring. If you only plan to run a refrigerator and a few lamps on extension cords plugged directly into the generator, a transfer switch is not required, but it is far more convenient and safer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a transfer switch and an interlock kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A manual transfer switch is a small prewired panel with its own switch for each chosen circuit, mounted beside the main panel. An interlock kit is a plate on the main panel that prevents the main breaker and a generator breaker from being on together, which makes the whole panel available. Interlocks usually cost less but must be listed for your specific panel, and you manage loads by switching breakers yourself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a generator transfer switch myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most places this is work for a licensed electrician, with a permit and inspection. It involves the main panel, where the incoming service conductors stay energized even with the main breaker off. Some jurisdictions allow homeowners to do electrical work on their own home with a permit, but transfer switches are a poor place to learn, because an error can backfeed the utility lines or create a fire hazard.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size transfer switch do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the switch to your generator's largest 240-volt outlet. A generator with an L14-30 outlet pairs with a 30-amp switch, such as the Reliance 31406CRK or 31410CRK. A generator with a 50-amp, 125/250-volt outlet can use a 50-amp inlet and a switch or interlock breaker sized for it. Then choose six or ten circuits based on how many essential loads you have.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a transfer switch for just a furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Single-circuit furnace transfer switches such as the Reliance TF151W mount at the furnace and let it run from either its normal circuit or a generator. They suit small or 120-volt-only generators, because a gas or oil furnace needs only a few hundred watts. Have an electrician confirm it suits your furnace, since some furnace controls are sensitive to generator grounding and polarity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a generator inlet box?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A generator inlet box is a weatherproof box mounted on an outside wall with a male inlet where the generator cord plugs in. It is wired to the transfer switch or interlock breaker inside. The Reliance PB30 takes a 30-amp L14-30 cord and the PBN50 takes a 50-amp cord. It lets you connect the generator without running cords through a window or door, which would let exhaust indoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a transfer switch work with an inverter generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the generator's outlets. Many inverter generators are 120-volt only, which cannot properly feed a standard 120/240-volt switch or run any 240-volt load. Larger inverter models with an L14-30 outlet work with a 30-amp switch like any other generator. For a small 120-volt inverter, a single-circuit furnace switch is often the better match. Ask your electrician before connecting anything.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

